questionText:
Without any factory, client code does this directly inside main():

if (choice == 1) {
    return new Circle();
} else if (choice == 2) {
    return new Square();
}

What's the core problem this creates as more shapes get added?

A Simple Factory centralizes creation like this:

class ShapeFactory {
    Shape getShape(int choice) {
        if (choice == 1) {
            return new Circle();
        } else if (choice == 2) {
            return new Square();
        } else {
            return new Rectangle();
        }
    }
}

What does this actually fix, and what does it NOT fix?

To reduce (not eliminate) the if-else problem, the notes sketch a hashmap-based version:

Map<Integer, Shape> map = new HashMap<>();
map.put(1, obj1);
map.put(2, obj2);
map.put(3, obj3);

Shape getObj(int choice) {
    return map.get(choice);
}

What specific improvement does this give over the if-else version, and what limitation remains?

Real class-22 code:

public abstract class Level {
    abstract IEnemy createEnemy();

    public void startLevel(){
        IEnemy enemy = createEnemy();
        enemy.attack();
    }
}

public class ForestLevel extends Level {
    @Override
    IEnemy createEnemy() {
        return new Goblin();
    }
}

Where does the actual new Goblin() call live, and why does startLevel() never need to know which enemy type it's creating?

Given Main.java:

Level forest = new SnowLevel();
forest.startLevel();

despite the variable being named forest, what actually gets attacked, and why?

Real class-22 code:

public interface FurnitureFactory {
    Chair createChair();
    Sofa createSofa();
}

public class ModernFurnitureFactory implements FurnitureFactory {
    @Override
    public Chair createChair() {
        return new ModernChair();
    }

    @Override
    public Sofa createSofa() {
        return new ModernSofa();
    }
}

What's the key structural difference between FurnitureFactory here and Level's single createEnemy() from Factory Method?

Given FurnitureStore:

public class FurnitureStore {
    private final FurnitureFactory factory;

    public FurnitureStore(FurnitureFactory factory) {
        this.factory = factory;
    }

    public void furnishRoom() {
        Chair chair = factory.createChair();
        Sofa sofa = factory.createSofa();
        chair.sitOn();
        sofa.lieOn();
    }
}

If FurnitureStore is constructed with new ModernFurnitureFactory(), is it possible for furnishRoom() to accidentally mix in a VictorianChair with a ModernSofa?

Main.java does:

FurnitureStore modernStore = new FurnitureStore(new ModernFurnitureFactory());
modernStore.furnishRoom();

What role does Main play here, and why is it significant that FurnitureStore's constructor takes FurnitureFactory factory (the interface) rather than ModernFurnitureFactory factory (the concrete class)?
